      "latency_ms": 33001.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1216,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance that is essential for life on Earth. It is a transparent, odorless, tasteless liquid that is the foundation of all known forms of life.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, broken down by its chemical, physical, and biological properties:\n\n### 1. Chemical Identity\n*   **Formula:** Water is chemically represented by the formula **$H_2O$**. This means each molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n*   **Polarity:** Water is a **polar molecule**. This means the oxygen atom pulls the shared electrons closer to itself, giving the oxygen a slight negative charge and the hydrogens a slight positive charge. This polarity is what allows water to stick to itself (surface tension) and to dissolve other substances.\n\n### 2. States of Matter\nWater is unique because it can exist naturally in all three states of matter under standard Earth conditions:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules move slowly and lock into a rigid crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid (Water):** This is the state we encounter most often. Molecules move freely but are still attracted to one another.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules gain enough energy to break free from the liquid and float into the air.\n\n### 3. Key Physical Properties\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Water is excellent at dissolving other substances. This is why it is the primary transport medium in the human body (blood) and in nature (rivers and oceans). It carries nutrients and waste products throughout living things.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot itself. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps human bodies from overheating.\n*   **Surface Tension:** Water has a high surface tension, which allows it to form droplets and \"skin\" on the surface of a liquid. This property is crucial for capillary action, which allows plants to pull water up from their roots.\n\n### 4. Abundance\nWater is the most abundant compound on the surface of the Earth. It covers about 71% of the planet and makes up about 60% of the human body. It is found in oceans, lakes, rivers, glaciers, underground aquifers, and even in the atmosphere as clouds."
